+# Contributing
+
+Thanks for contributing. This repository accepts additions and corrections only when they meet the same standards as the existing list.
+
+## Submission Rules
+
+- Every tool must have a public `github.com` repository URL.
+- No proprietary SaaS products, closed-source tools, or vendor-only landing pages.
+- Prefer tools that are self-hostable, extensible, or meaningfully open-source.
+- Include the exact GitHub repo URL, stars, primary language, license, and a recent last-commit date.
+- Explain the insurance workflow the tool supports: examples include document intake, policy search, claims triage, customer support, CRM, transcription, or workflow automation.
+- If a category has very few good fits, say so instead of padding the list with weak matches.
+
+## Pull Request Format
+
+When opening a PR, include:
+
+1. Repository name and URL.
+2. One short paragraph describing what the tool does.
+3. GitHub metadata snapshot:
+   - Stars
+   - Primary language
+   - License
+   - Last commit date
+4. Best-fit category for this awesome list.
+5. Why it is useful for insurance agents specifically.
+
+## Quality Bar
+
+- Keep descriptions factual and based on the repository README or GitHub repository page.
+- Do not submit forks unless the fork is independently maintained and materially different.
+- Do not submit inactive or abandoned repos unless there is a compelling reason and it is clearly labeled.
+- Do not remove the GitHub URL requirement.
+
+## Reporting Fixes
+
+Open an issue or pull request if:
+
+- A repo was archived, deleted, or relicensed.
+- GitHub metadata drifted significantly.
+- A better open-source repo exists for the same insurance workflow.
+- A category should be marked as a gap because there are fewer than 5 credible repos.
